From d34422c4c861113e2bded841f6edae54385aa60b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 09:16:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Matica Print Flow: Print Agent token, resolve/record backend
 calls, dead code removal

Implements the Matica Service side of the agreed plan:

- Short-lived Print Agent token validation: a dedicated JWT bearer scheme
  (PrintAgentAuthOptions - separate signing key from anything else, matching
  the Inventory API's PrintAgentToken:SigningKey exactly) now guards every
  action on MachineController via [Authorize(Policy = PrintAgentOnly)].
  Program.cs previously called UseAuthorization() with no authentication
  scheme registered at all, which was a no-op; this is a real fix, not
  just an addition.
- Backend Call #1 (IPrintFlowClient.ResolveForPrintAsync) fires right after
  ReadMAG, using the raw PAN transiently - never logged, never referenced
  again after the call returns.
- Backend Call #2 (RecordPrintResultAsync) fires right after EjectCard,
  deliberately reordered from the old Emboss-then-log sequence so the
  physical card reaches the operator before the retry loop runs. Retries
  a bounded number of times on a transient failure (network/timeout/5xx)
  reusing the same idempotency key; never retries a well-formed business
  rejection, since resending it won't change the answer. A "printed but
  could not confirm with the backend" outcome is surfaced as its own
  distinct response (HTTP 207), not swallowed into an ordinary failure.
- This service still owns zero business logic: it calls the Inventory
  API's two print-flow endpoints and reacts to what they say. It never
  sees the Known-way/Unknown-way distinction.
- Removed the four legacy business lookup calls (MachineConfigrations,
  PrintConfigurations, Products, Branch) - Angular now supplies
  ProductId/BranchId (longs, not names - the Inventory API's own name
  filters are substring matches, not exact) and print-layout fields
  directly, since it already has them from its own Inventory API browsing.
- Removed GetChaeckCardExist/SetPrintLogAsync (API_Handle.cs) and their
  supporting API_HttpClient.cs/APIHelper.cs - both fully superseded.
- JSON-based configuration: WebAPI string-indexer replaced with
  strongly-typed InventoryApiOptions/PrintAgentAuthOptions (Options
  pattern), each with the same fail-fast startup validation convention
  the Inventory API already uses for its own signing key.
- Status-parsing fix: MachineInfoJSON now has [JsonProperty] mappings and
  is actually deserialized and checked, instead of raw string.Contains(...)
  against the response text. Only machine_status/card_inside/tipper_status
  are confirmed wire field names (matching the exact strings the old code
  string-matched on); every other mapped field is a best-guess snake_case
  conversion, explicitly flagged as unconfirmed in the class doc comment -
  verify against a real device capture before relying on those.
- Replaced the last real use of Nancy (JavaScriptSerializer in
  MachineCommands.httpPOST) with the JsonConvert already used everywhere
  else in that file, and removed the Nancy package. Replaced the dead,
  commented-out Parser.GetInfoParsing reference (confirmed absent anywhere
  in this codebase after repeated search) with real deserialization into
  MachineInfoJSON.
- Logger fix: now a singleton (registered once in Program.cs) instead of
  constructed per request on MachineController - fixes the log-file race
  from per-request Mutex instances that never serialized against each
  other, and stops the log-directory scan-and-delete from running on
  every single request.
- Removed everything left entirely dead by the above: the large
  commented-out duplicate Print block, the Params classes and AUB enums
  with no remaining reference anywhere in the repo (confirmed by
  repo-wide grep before deletion, not assumed), and one real bug caught
  while rewriting - the old happy-path return was
  'Ok(new { success = false, ... })'.

Deliberately NOT included, flagged rather than silently dropped: the full
async conversion of MachineCommands' hardware I/O (httpPOST/
httpPOSTGetInfoJson/CommandManagement still use ref parameters and
blocking HttpWebRequest calls, unchanged). Converting these requires
removing ref from four methods across roughly thirty call sites, and the
prior review already flagged this specific change as needing
hardware-in-the-loop validation before rollout - not something to do
blind, without a compiler, in the same pass as everything else above.

Build-error fix (post-review): the first version of this patch dropped
'using AUBServicesLayer.Enums;' from MachineController.cs while rewriting
the using block, breaking every 'enumType.Error' reference (CS0103,
reported against real Visual Studio build output). Fixed, and used the
opportunity to re-verify the rest of the patch properly instead of
eyeballing it a second time: built an actual symbol table (every public
type in this project mapped to its declaring namespace, correctly
handling the UTF-8 BOM on several files that had silently broken the
first pass's regex) and cross-checked every modified file's using
directives against it programmatically. That caught a second latent
issue before it reached you - Program.cs's AddOptions<T>().Bind()
.Validate().ValidateOnStart() chain needs Microsoft.Extensions.Options
explicitly (extension methods require the namespace in scope; confirmed
against the Inventory API's own InfrastructureServiceRegistration.cs,
which uses the identical pattern and imports it) - now added, along with
Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization defensively (AddPolicy/RequireClaim
are almost certainly plain instance methods that wouldn't strictly need
it, but there was no reason to leave that as a third guess).

No .NET SDK or NuGet access available in the authoring environment
(confirmed: nuget.org is not in the sandbox's allowed domain list), so
none of this has actually been compiled. Verified by the symbol-table
cross-check described above, brace-balance checks across every touched
file, and JSON/XML well-formedness checks on appsettings.json and the
.csproj. This patch applying cleanly via git am is not the same as it
compiling - please run a full build locally before merging, and validate
Backend Call #2's retry/reorder behavior and the [JsonProperty] guesses
against real hardware before rollout.
